Yup. I did it. After nearly a decade I finally did it.

So just this once I hope you'll forgive me if I wax verbose.

After not playing for most of this year, I got the urge this week, so hit
up a couple of random starts. The first was a quick death; this was the
second.

Klepsie and her little dog Sirius had an uneventful start, making it down
as far as Minetown with her trusty crossbow. Minetown was good and bad;
good in that the altar was co-aligned, bad in that the shopping there was
scarcely going to challenge Oxford Street -- apart from Izchak, there was
one tiny hardware store and that was that! Luckily there was a largeish
scroll shop on level 3 (no shop on level 2, thanks to the RNG putting the
downstair in the only possible room), and an armour store on level 5,
whence came speed boots.

Sirius died in Minetown through trying to take on an entire regiment of
Mordor orcs, but I sacrificed at the altar there until I got... Mjollnir?
Hm. Not what I'd have given a Ranger if I were Venus, but mustn't complain.
I decided to leave the rest of the Mines for later.

The Oracle level had my first bones file. As usual, pretty much everything
useful was cursed, except an amulet of reflection which I promptly glued
round my neck (where it stayed for the entire game, I think). And
thankfully, Sokoban gave me a bag of holding to go with it.

And so down I went, through a Big Room and a Rogue Level which provided no
problems, aiming to reach level 14 and get onto the quest. All the way to
Medusa's level, where I had to halt because I had no easy means of crossing
the water -- no boots, no ring, no wand of cold.

Thankfully someone here dropped a !oGL which finally made me 14, so off I
went back to the quest. Which was frankly pretty easy. And also yielded me
a ring of levitation, enabling me to dispose of Medusa and progress to the
Castle. There was also another co-aligned altar around here with a priest
who was happy to take money off me in return for protection. Lucky he
didn't see the gold was all stamped 'Property of Leprechauns! Hands Off!'.

Blowing a dead soldier's bugle to work the drawbridge, Mjollnir made pretty
short work of everyone who came spilling out to see who was making that
dreadful racket. Having cleared the castle out, I finally seized the wand
of wishing. First wish: GDSM to replace the elven mithril I'd been making
do with. Second wish, because for some reason scrolls of identify were very
rare (even in the scroll shop), a spellbook of identify... which I found I
couldn't cast. So next up came a robe so I could wear it and at least stand
a chance of identifying all those things. Not an elegant solution, I know.

The castle throne kindly gave me an extinction; I wiped out arch liches.

Thus armed with identify, I headed back up to the bones pile by the Oracle
to see what else I could raid from that. And here came my first mistake...
I knew wands of cancellation shouldn't go into bags of holding, I really
did. But even Homer nods from time to time, and I certainly did...

I almost gave up there and then, especially as my wand of wishing went
west, but shrugged figuratively and decided I might as well go on down.
After all I had a decent set of armour and two good weapons.


I was relatively lucky in Gehennom's layout, in that upstairs and
downstairs were often close to each other. I paid Asmodeus off without
blushing, put paid to Juiblex with a few whacks from Mjollnir, and then got
luckier still when I reached Baalzebub; he wasn't at home. Yeenoghu was,
and so was my second ghost. All I can assume is that the bones pile was a
result of my killing off Baalzebub but then succumbing to Yeenoghu instead,
whom he'd gated in.

Disposing of Yeenoghu I plundered the bones pile here. No less than three
artefacts; Stormbringer, the Heart of Ahriman and Cleaver. But since I had
been wielding Mjollnir with such good results, I stuck with it.

Luck also played a part in providing me with several wands of death around
here. Orcus's was not one of them, but a number of other monsters were kind
enough to drop them between now and the bottom. 

What happened to Orcus's, you ask? Well, I arrived on his level by falling
through a trap rather than the more orthodox means, and landed almost on
top of him. After a few wallops with Mjollnir, his /oDeath broke apart and
exploded, and he ran off upstairs. I left him cowering there to take care
of on my way back.

Vlad was a pushover.

And then came my next tremendous slice of luck; another bones file, of all
places, on the middle level of the Wizard's tower (in the little room to
the left of the zoo). Whatever killed me the previous time wasn't still
around (I suspect it may have been Yeenoghu again, looking at my high score
chart). And -- thank you, thank you, RNG -- the bag of holding in the bones
pile was still blessed. 7 candles, ka-ching. T shirt, ka-ching. Gauntlets
of Power, ka-ching. Artefacts, ka-ching. Three magic markers, ka-ching (all
empty though). I sat there patiently identifying everything, weeding out
the dross, for what seemed like years. I had about seven amulets of
reflection by now, and had to leave some stacked in a heap to keep weight
down.

Thus armed it didn't take too much effort to cosh the Wizard of Yendor and
steal his Book of the Dead.

On down to level 49 where the vibrating square was found after a lot of
pacing around. The ritual was performed, and I entered Moloch's Sanctum.
This was annoying; monsters kept being summoned, and it was awkward to
fight them because there was a fire trap right inside the door between the
two halves of the level. Things got ticklish enough that I had to use my
one scroll of genocide to rid me of a lich who was beating me up. Also, at
one point my unicorn horn got cursed, which I only found out when I was
blinded... It took forever to wear off. But eventually I managed to
penetrate the inner sanctum and kill the occupant. 

Of course, now came my second biggest blunder; on my way out I managed to
fall into the moat round the vibrating square and blanked a lot of my
scrolls.

I was on entirely unfamiliar turf now, and having either never read or else
forgotten some spoilers, I wasn't prepared for things like a mysterious
force that kept dragging me back to level 38, or the Wizard coming back
from the dead for revenge (luckily he only managed to grab the amulet once,
the very first time this happened). I struggled out of Gehennom (killing
Orcus en route) and past Medusa's island, and there I stopped for the
night, having first made a post here asking for help (many thanks to those
who answered!)

Next day in realtime, I acted on advice received. I dipped a lot of water
to make holy water, something I never knew you could do. I wrote scrolls to
enchant armour. I quaffed a bunch of potions to increase my hit points. And
up, at last, I went, pausing every few levels to kill the Wizard again, and
onto the plane of earth.

The Wizard instantly appeared again, duplicated himself, and surrounded me
with monsters. Even liberal use of my wands of death couldn't stop a master
mind flayer from glomping onto me and sucking my brain, which I discovered
to my dismay meant I forgot the ID of certain items as well as losing IQ.
Fortunately I still retained most of the important ones... such as wands of
digging, which I wandered round the level with till I fell over a portal.

Air wasn't too bad; the air elementals were a bit mean, but there were
plenty of steam and energy vortices I could hide away inside and
recuperate. I didn't take too long to stumble over the portal either.

Fire was simpler still, since a cobra triggered the portal almost as soon
as I got to the level and all I had to do was fight and levitate my way
over to it.

Water... was nothing at all like I expected. But after a bit my bubble
wandered over the portal and I was through. One more step!

I arrived to the right on the Astral Plane, so went that way. The first
thing that happened was a master lich quaffing a potion of gain level and
dropping dead due to arch liches being genocided, which I took as a good
omen. Famine loomed up ahead of me, but my wand of death put paid to him
every time he tried to cluster round me. Wrong altar, though. I fought my
way through swarms of insects toward the middle altar (losing my angel
somewhere en route -- he didn't die, but got stuck somewhere and I never
saw him again, and I'd thrown away my magic whistle to lighten the load.)

The middle altar was Death's. I knew not to zap him, but didn't know what
else to do instead, other than wallop him. So I did. And to my surprise he
turned out to be fairly wussy (especially the first kill, where a helpful
priestess of Mercury kept shooting magic missiles at me that bounced off
into him). He knocked my max HP down a few times, but potions put it back
up some of the way. But... wrong altar again!

So off to the left I went through more insects. Pestilence got the wand of
death treatment and never touched me. A real annoyance, though, was Steve
the Archaeologist. I have no idea who he is (or rather was...) but he was
standing in the gateway to the temple smacking me with Vorpal Blade, and
took a hell of a lot of beating down. I didn't dare zap him to death
because the priest was behind him and I had horrid visions of killing the
priest and making the game unwinnable. (Can you do this?)

Eventually I finished him off and picked up Vorpal Blade. +8, greased. No
wonder he was such a hard hitter! (That made *eleven* artefacts I was
toting around at the end.)

And so to the altar. Chat with the priest, receive two bits for an ale...
and finally... finally!

I never got to Fort Ludios, nor did I ever meet the mysterious Rodney who
I've heard some people have trouble with on the home run, but you know
what? I don't care!


You offer the Amulet of Yendor to Venus...--More--



